      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "8 1. To the extent that the crimes set forth in article 2 are not listed as",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976#article-8"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976.pdf, p.20",
      "values:originalText": "extraditable offences in any extradition treaty existing between States Parties,\nthey shall be deemed to be included as such therein. States Parties undertake to\n\nConvention on the prevention and punishment of crimes against internationally\nprotected persons, including diplomatic agents  Schedule\n\nCrimes (Internationally Protected Persons) Act 1976\nCompilation date: 10/3/16\nRegistered: 26/5/16\n\ninclude those crimes as extraditable offences in every future extradition treaty to\nbe concluded between them.\n2. If a State Party which makes extradition conditional on the existence of a\ntreaty receives a request for extradition from another State Party with which it\nhas no extradition treaty, it may, if it decides to extradite, consider this\nConvention as the legal basis for extradition in respect of those crimes.\nExtradition shall be subject to the procedural provisions and the other\nconditions of the law of the requested State.\n3. States Parties which do not make extradition conditional on the existence\nof a treaty shall recognize those crimes as extraditable offences between\nthemselves subject to the procedural provisions and the other conditions of the\nlaw of the requested State.\n4. Each of the crimes shall be treated, for the purpose of extradition between\nStates Parties, as if it had been committed not only in the place in which it\noccurred but also in the territories of the States required to establish their\njurisdiction in accordance with paragraph 1 of article 3.",
      "skos:definition": "extraditable offences in any extradition treaty existing between States Parties,\nthey shall be deemed to be included as such therein. States Parties undertake to\n\nConvention on the prevention and punishment of crimes against internationally\nprotected persons, including diplomatic agents  Schedule\n\nCrimes (Internationally Protected Persons) Act 1976\nCompilation date: 10/3/16\nRegistered: 26/5/16\n\ninclude those crimes as extraditable offences in every future extradition treaty to\nbe concluded between them.\n2. If a State Party which makes extradition conditional on the existence of a\ntreaty receives a request for extradition from another State Party with which it\nhas no extradition treaty, it may, if it decides to extradite, consider this\nConvention as the legal basis for extradition in respect of those crimes.\nExtradition shall be subject to the procedural provisions and the other\nconditions of the law of the requested State.\n3. States Parties which do not make extradition conditional on the existence\nof a treaty shall recognize those crimes as extraditable offences between\nthemselves subject to the procedural provisions and the other conditions of the\nlaw of the requested State.\n4. Each of the crimes shall be treated, for the purpose of extradition between\nStates Parties, as if it had been committed not only in the place in which it\noccurred but also in the territories of the States required to establish their\njurisdiction in accordance with paragraph 1 of article 3.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "46674d3a29907c6e7d142e159167919524af2fc04573b8102c09e74b4e4e6a29",
      "skos:note": "States Parties undertake to include crimes as extraditable offences in future extradition treaties."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976#article-8",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "8 1. To the extent that the crimes set forth in article 2 are not listed as",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 20,
      "values:originalText": "extraditable offences in any extradition treaty existing between States Parties,\nthey shall be deemed to be included as such therein. States Parties undertake to\n\nConvention on the prevention and punishment of crimes against internationally\nprotected persons, including diplomatic agents  Schedule\n\nCrimes (Internationally Protected Persons) Act 1976\nCompilation date: 10/3/16\nRegistered: 26/5/16\n\ninclude those crimes as extraditable offences in every future extradition treaty to\nbe concluded between them.\n2. If a State Party which makes extradition conditional on the existence of a\ntreaty receives a request for extradition from another State Party with which it\nhas no extradition treaty, it may, if it decides to extradite, consider this\nConvention as the legal basis for extradition in respect of those crimes.\nExtradition shall be subject to the procedural provisions and the other\nconditions of the law of the requested State.\n3. States Parties which do not make extradition conditional on the existence\nof a treaty shall recognize those crimes as extraditable offences between\nthemselves subject to the procedural provisions and the other conditions of the\nlaw of the requested State.\n4. Each of the crimes shall be treated, for the purpose of extradition between\nStates Parties, as if it had been committed not only in the place in which it\noccurred but also in the territories of the States required to establish their\njurisdiction in accordance with paragraph 1 of article 3."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8-norm",
      "@type": "values:Undertaking",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-8-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "undertaking",
      "cml:logicSummary": "States Parties undertake to include crimes as extraditable offences.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-9",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "9 Any person regarding whom proceedings are being carried out in connexion",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976#article-9"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
