    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-13",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "13 1. Any dispute between two or more States Parties concerning the",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976#article-13"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976.pdf, p.22",
      "values:originalText": "interpretation or application of this Convention which is not settled by\nnegotiation shall, at the request of one of them, be submitted to arbitration. If\nwithin six months from the date of the request for arbitration the parties are\nunable to agree on the organization of the arbitration, any one of those parties\nmay refer the dispute to the International Court of Justice by request in\nconformity with the Statute of the Court.\n2. Each State Party may at the time of signature or ratification of this\nConvention or accession thereto declare that it does not consider itself bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article. The other States Parties shall not be bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article with respect to any State Party which has made such a\nreservation.\n3. Any State Party which has made a reservation in accordance with\nparagraph 2 of this article may at any time withdraw that reservation by\nnotification to the Secretary-General of the United Nations.",
      "skos:definition": "interpretation or application of this Convention which is not settled by\nnegotiation shall, at the request of one of them, be submitted to arbitration. If\nwithin six months from the date of the request for arbitration the parties are\nunable to agree on the organization of the arbitration, any one of those parties\nmay refer the dispute to the International Court of Justice by request in\nconformity with the Statute of the Court.\n2. Each State Party may at the time of signature or ratification of this\nConvention or accession thereto declare that it does not consider itself bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article. The other States Parties shall not be bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article with respect to any State Party which has made such a\nreservation.\n3. Any State Party which has made a reservation in accordance with\nparagraph 2 of this article may at any time withdraw that reservation by\nnotification to the Secretary-General of the United Nations.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-13-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976-article-13-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "815d2c951b11b100a653b8d97c1d2ccfbb88bf08f2087e6755a3e4ab8e7e28d5",
      "skos:note": "States Parties must submit disputes to arbitration or the International Court of Justice."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-internationally-protected-persons-act-1976#article-13",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "13 1. Any dispute between two or more States Parties concerning the",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 22,
      "values:originalText": "interpretation or application of this Convention which is not settled by\nnegotiation shall, at the request of one of them, be submitted to arbitration. If\nwithin six months from the date of the request for arbitration the parties are\nunable to agree on the organization of the arbitration, any one of those parties\nmay refer the dispute to the International Court of Justice by request in\nconformity with the Statute of the Court.\n2. Each State Party may at the time of signature or ratification of this\nConvention or accession thereto declare that it does not consider itself bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article. The other States Parties shall not be bound by\nparagraph 1 of this article with respect to any State Party which has made such a\nreservation.\n3. Any State Party which has made a reservation in accordance with\nparagraph 2 of this article may at any time withdraw that reservation by\nnotification to the Secretary-General of the United Nations."
    },
